{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "03-systems.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Solving systems by elimination",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Find which input a matrix sends to a given output by elimination on the augmented matrix, and see when there is one answer, none, or infinitely many.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lesson 2 ran a matrix forwards. Now run one backwards: $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}$ sends some $(x, y)$ to $(5, 6)$. Which?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The matrix sends $(x, y)$ to $x$ times its first column plus $y$ times its second. So the question is which combination of the columns makes $(5, 6)$; entry by entry, it is two equations, $x + 2y = 5$ and $3x + 4y = 6$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "[1, 2; 3, 4]*[x; y]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Elimination",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "By hand you would eliminate: take $3$ times the first equation from the second, so that $x$ drops out of it; solve that for $y$; put it back. Only the numbers matter, so write the system as one table, the **augmented matrix**: one row per equation, the right-hand sides in the last column, `[1, 2, 5; 3, 4, 6]`.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Each step below is a move on the equations: swap two, scale one by a nonzero number, or add a multiple of one to another. Before stepping, predict the first move and what the second row becomes.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "rref([1, 2, 5; 3, 4, 6])",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "The result is in **reduced row echelon form**: each variable stands alone in its own row. The first row says $x = -4$, the second $y = \\frac92$. Run it forwards to check:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "[1, 2; 3, 4]*[-4; 9/2]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Why do the moves never lose the answer, or make up a new one? Draw the equations. The points $(x, y)$ with $x + 2y = 5$ form a line, and so do those with $3x + 4y = 6$; the solution is where the two lines cross.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock t from 0 to 2\nview -7.5, 3.5, -0.8, 6.8\nlet a2 = 3 - 3*t\nlet b2 = 4 - 6*t\nlet c2 = 6 - 15*t\nlet b1 = 4 - 2*t\nlet c1 = 14 - 9*t\nL1 = line([1, 2], [-1, 3]) thick color 3\nL2 = line(c2/(a2^2 + b2^2)*[a2, b2], c2/(a2^2 + b2^2)*[a2, b2] + [-b2, a2]) thick color 4\nL2b = line([0, 4.5], [1, 4.5]) thick color 4\nL1m = line(c1/(1 + b1^2)*[1, b1], c1/(1 + b1^2)*[1, b1] + [-b1, 1]) thick color 3\nX = point([-4, 4.5]) thick color 1\nLX = label([-4, 4.5], \"(-4, \\tfrac92)\") color 1\n> show L1, L2, X, LX | The two equations as lines: $x + 2y = 5$ (green) and $3x + 4y = 6$ (pink). The solution is where they cross.\n> play t to 1 in 3s | Take $3$ times the first row from the second, a little at a time. Part way, the row is $3x + 4y - s\\,(x + 2y) = 6 - 5s$, true wherever both equations are: the pink line turns about the crossing, and ends level, $-2y = -9$.\n> hide L2; show L2b | Scale the second row by $-\\frac12$: $y = \\frac92$. The same line, written more simply.\n> hide L1; show L1m; play t to 2 in 3s | Take twice the second row from the first. Now the green line turns, about the same point, until it stands upright: $x = -4$.\n> | Reduced, each line names one coordinate, and the crossing never moved: $x = -4$, $y = \\frac92$.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "So no move loses the solution: a combination of the equations holds wherever both do, and its line goes through the crossing. Nor does a move make up a new one, because each move can be undone, and undoing it would lose that new solution.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] Row operations keep the solutions\n> Swapping two rows, scaling a row by a nonzero number and adding a multiple of one row to another do not change the set of solutions. Each move can be undone by another (swap back, scale by the reciprocal, subtract the multiple again), so no solution is gained and none is lost. For rational entries each of the three is proved in Lean, as is elimination as a whole and the claim that its result is in reduced row echelon form: the green dots on the steps.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Scaling by $0$ is the one move left out, because it cannot be undone: it turns an equation into $0 = 0$ and forgets it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Three equations",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Three unknowns, three equations, the same moves: $2x + y - z = 8$, $-3x - y + 2z = -11$, $-2x + y + 2z = -3$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "rref([2, 1, -1, 8; -3, -1, 2, -11; -2, 1, 2, -3])",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "So $x = 2$, $y = 3$, $z = -1$. Forwards again, all three equations at once:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "[2, 1, -1; -3, -1, 2; -2, 1, 2]*[2; 3; -1]",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "When it fails",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Not every matrix can be run backwards. $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}$ sends $\\hat{\\imath}$ to $(1, 2)$ and $\\hat{\\jmath}$ to $(2, 4)$, in the same direction. Watch the plane:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "clock t from 0 to 1\nview -3, 6, -2, 8\nlet M = (1 - t)*[1, 0; 0, 1] + t*[1, 2; 2, 4]\nG0 = grid([1, 0; 0, 1]) faint color 6\nG = grid(M) color 2\nQ = poly(0, M*[1; 0], M*[1; 1], M*[0; 1]) color 5\nK = line(0, [1, 2]) dashed color 6\nI = arrow(0, M*[1; 0]) thick color 3\nJ = arrow(0, M*[0; 1]) thick color 4\nP1 = point(M*[3; 0]) color 1\nP2 = point(M*[1; 1]) color 1\nP3 = point(M*[-1; 2]) color 1\nT = point([3, 7]) thick color 6\nLT = label([3, 7], \"(3, 7)\") color 6\n> wait 1s | The grid, the unit square on $\\hat{\\imath}$ (green) and $\\hat{\\jmath}$ (pink), and three points: $(3, 0)$, $(1, 1)$ and $(-1, 2)$.\n> show K; play t to 1 in 4s | Apply the matrix: $\\hat{\\imath}$ goes to $(1, 2)$, $\\hat{\\jmath}$ to $(2, 4)$, and the whole plane is squashed onto the line $y = 2x$, the square to a segment of it. All three points land on $(3, 6)$.\n> show T, LT | Nothing lands on $(3, 7)$, off the line.",
      "type": "scene",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Every output lies on one line. A target on it, such as $(3, 6)$, is hit by a whole line of inputs; a target off it, such as $(3, 7)$, by none. Elimination finds both:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "rref([1, 2, 3; 2, 4, 6])",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A row of zeros: the second equation was twice the first, and only $x + 2y = 3$ is left, a whole line of solutions.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "rref([1, 2, 3; 2, 4, 7])",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The last row says $0x + 0y = 1$: no solution.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "rref([1, 1, 3; 1, -1, 1])",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Solve $x + y = 3,\\ x - y = 1$ by reducing the augmented matrix. Give the reduced matrix.",
      "hints": [
        "The augmented matrix is `[1, 1, 3; 1, -1, 1]`.",
        "Subtract the first row from the second, giving $0, -2, -2$; scale that by $-\\frac12$; then subtract it from the first row."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "rref([2, 4, 6; 1, 3, 4])",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce the augmented matrix of $2x + 4y = 6,\\ x + 3y = 4$.",
      "hints": [
        "Scale the first row by $\\frac12$ first."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "rref([3, 1, 5; 1, 2, 5])",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Lesson 1 asked for $a$ and $b$ with $a\\,(3, 1) + b\\,(1, 2) = (5, 5)$, found by dragging. Find them by elimination: give the reduced augmented matrix.",
      "hints": [
        "The columns are $(3, 1)$, $(1, 2)$ and the target $(5, 5)$: `[3, 1, 5; 1, 2, 5]`."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "rref([1, -1, 2; -2, 2, 1])",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Reduce the augmented matrix of $x - y = 2,\\ -2x + 2y = 1$. How many solutions are there?",
      "hints": [
        "Add twice the first row to the second.",
        "A row $0, 0, c$ with $c \\neq 0$ says $0 = c$."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Solving $A\\,x = b$ asks which input $A$ sends to $b$. Elimination answers it with three moves on the augmented matrix, each of which can be undone and so keeps the solutions, until each variable stands alone. A matrix that squashes the plane onto a line cannot be run backwards: a target on the line has infinitely many inputs, one off it has none.\n>\n> How can you tell from the matrix alone, before solving anything, whether it squashes the plane? Lesson 4 measures what it does to area.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
